# The image where porthole is **installed**, not driven by hand.
#
# Every other image here runs `porthole-helper --session` as a child process
# of the test's own shell, which is the only shape available without an init
# system. That shape cannot answer the questions this image exists for,
# because all of them are questions about systemd:
#
#   - a `Type=dbus` unit that gives up its `BusName` is sent `SIGTERM` within
#     microseconds, measured, and a helper that did not handle it would be
#     killed in the middle of its own retirement;
#   - what systemd *records* when the helper exits by itself -- `Result=`,
#     `NRestarts=`, and whether anything lands at warning level or above;
#   - whether repeated activate-and-exit cycles trip the start rate limiter,
#     which counts failed starts and must not count clean ones;
#   - whether the bus really re-activates the unit after it has gone.
#
# So this one carries systemd as PID 1, a system bus, and firewalld, and the
# test installs the shipped `data/` files into the paths the packages use.
# No Rust toolchain and no copy of the binaries: those are built once on the
# host as static musl binaries and bind-mounted in at container-run time,
# exactly as the other four images do it.
#
# `polkit` **does** start here, and that is worth saying because it is not
# free and the spike that preceded this image could not manage it: polkit
# failed there with `Failed to keep CAP_SYS_ADMIN`, status `217/USER`, so
# every measurement in it ran with no authorizer anywhere. What it takes is
# what `SystemdContainer::start` already passes for the Docker image --
# `--cap-add=...SYS_ADMIN...` together with `--security-opt seccomp=unconfined`
# -- and the setup in `container.rs` asserts it rather than hoping, ending
# with `systemctl is-active polkit` under `set -e`.
#
# That is the main thing this image newly buys. Two of the tests using it
# drive a real `porthole open` and `porthole close` through the shipped
# `com.jacopobriccola.Porthole.policy`, so the `auth_admin` path is exercised
# against a real firewalld rather than stepped around. The container adds one
# polkit rule granting root those actions, because `auth_admin` needs an
# authentication agent to answer the prompt and a container has none; the
# shipped policy itself is installed unmodified and is still what decides.
#
# The two calls that need no authorizer -- `org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer.Ping`
# and `com.jacopobriccola.Porthole1.ProtocolVersion` -- are used by the tests
# whose subject is activation itself, so that what those measure is the bus
# starting a helper and nothing else.
FROM registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora:44

RUN dnf install -y --setopt=install_weak_deps=False \
      systemd \
      dbus-broker \
      dbus-tools \
      firewalld \
      iproute \
      nftables \
      polkit \
      procps-ng \
    && dnf clean all \
    && systemctl enable dbus-broker.service

# Nothing about porthole is baked in: the unit, the D-Bus activation file,
# the bus policy and the polkit actions are installed by the test from this
# repository's own `data/` directory, so what runs here is the shipped
# arrangement and not a second copy of it that could drift.
CMD ["/sbin/init"]
